WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.790
Film criticism is definitely worthwhile, but
let’s be honest here: sometimes critics

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:08.960
get so caught up in whether or not a movie
is “good” that they stop considering whether

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.480
it’s fun to watch — and yes, there is
a difference.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.900
From swashbuckling adventure to action-packed
sci-fi, here are some movies that the critics

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.480
despised, but are actually pretty awesome.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.289
If you haven't seen them yet, beware of spoilers
ahead.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.669
Hook

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:26.599
It seems like everybody hates Hook.

00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:32.090
Film critics have savaged this 1991 Peter
Pan story as “embarrassingly excessive,”

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:36.629
“bloated,” and “a huge disappointment,”
and it’s widely considered to be Steven

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:38.100
Spielberg’s worst film.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.610
But maybe that's because all those critics
have grown up and forgotten what it's like

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.890
to have an imagination.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.899
Granted, Hook is nowhere close to being one
of Spielberg's best, but for a film about

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:52.690
childhood, it deals with some surprisingly
mature themes, like unrequited love and the

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.350
inevitability of death.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.739
The story stars Robin Williams, playing Peter
Pan as an adult who changed his name, started

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:04.750
a family, became a lawyer, and totally forgot
about his adventures in Neverland until his

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.710
children were kidnapped by Captain Hook.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.860
It's a film about dysfunctional families and
abandoned kids, and according to Evan Saathoff

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.390
of Birth.Movies.Death., Hook was a "very personal
movie for Spielberg, a reaction to his fears

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.000
that his heavy workload was robbing him of
time with his children."

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.860
In addition to the deep stuff going on behind
the surface, Hook is pure Spielberg-ian entertainment.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:32.899
The sets—which are quite obviously sets—are
grand, ornate, and wonderfully larger-than-life,

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:35.670
which is fitting for a story that was first
a stage play.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.320
Plus, you can't deny that Bob Hoskins, Maggie
Smith, and Dustin Hoffman are absolutely perfect

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.890
in their parts, and the score by John Williams
is pure magic.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.520
Alien 3

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.320
When first-time director David Fincher was
hired for Alien 3, the movie was already behind

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.320
schedule.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:58.840
There wasn't a finished script, and the studio
had lost several million dollars on the project.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.811
Worse still, Fincher was severely hampered
by micro-managing producers at Fox, and when

00:02:03.811 --> 00:02:08.300
the film clocked in around three hours long,
the studio forced him to cut about 30 minutes

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.440
of material.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:15.020
As a result of that bad experience, Fincher
disavowed the 1992 film, but he wasn't the

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.390
only one who hated Alien 3.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:22.800
Fans, and James Cameron, are still furious
about the deaths of Hicks and Newt, and most

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.280
critics panned the film, with many saying
it was style over substance.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.580
It's true that Alien 3 puts a lot of emphasis
on atmosphere, but at the same time, that's

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.470
what makes it one of the most fascinating
movies of Fincher's storied career.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:41.220
Set in a dreary wasteland that screams Mad
Max, this third installment is dark, grungy,

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.860
and beautifully barren cinematic nightmare.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.630
Sigourney Weaver is at the top of her game
here, playing a more cynical Ripley who bravely

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.590
comes to terms with her inevitable fate.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.650
True, the Xenomorph effects look a little
janky, and the forced edits take away from

00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:01.120
the overall story, but despite the drawbacks,
Alien 3 is a visually brilliant world of criminal

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.260
monks and fiery hellscapes, a film that could've
been the perfect ending for a landmark series.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.260
National Treasure

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:14.270
2004's National Treasure was described by
critics as "rancid cinematic cheese" with

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:19.200
“almost nothing to recommend it,” but
that's more than a little bit harsh.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.130
The film follows Nicolas Cage as Benjamin
Franklin Gates, a historian who learns that

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.200
there's a treasure map hidden on the back
of the Declaration of Independence.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.000
Unfortunately, fellow treasure hunter Ian
How, played by Sean Bean), decides to steal

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.590
the Declaration, forcing Ben and his sidekick
Riley to break into the National Archives

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.980
and get their hands on the document first.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.530
With the Declaration in hand, Ben sets out
across the stomping grounds of America's Founding

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.470
Fathers, solving riddles and doing his best
to avoid a barrage of bullets.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:53.600
It’s easy to argue that Cage was miscast
as the swashbuckling lead of a popcorn adventure

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.590
flick, and somebody definitely should've realized
there were 56 signers of the Declaration of

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:03.790
Independence, not 55, but National Treasure
really shines when our heroes are puzzling

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.570
over codes and trying to piece together clues.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:11.290
There's invisible ink, hidden ciphers, and
glasses invented by Benjamin Franklin, not

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.150
to mention conspiracies involving the Masons
and the Knights Templar.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.810
Plus, Sean Bean, Diane Kruger, and Harvey
Keitel are all fun to watch as they help and

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:25.479
hinder Cage in his quest to find the world's
biggest pile of gold.

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:27.289
RoboCop

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:31.620
Remaking a classic is always an uphill battle,
especially when that classic is a beloved

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.780
sci-fi masterpiece like RoboCop.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.280
Jose Padilha's 21st century remake doesn't
stand a chance against Paul Verhoeven's 1987

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.500
original, but the 2014 version is definitely
a film with its own style and its own ideas.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:51.570
Joel Kinnaman stars as Alex Murphy, a Detroit
cop who's been mortally wounded by a car bomb.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.919
With his corpse mangled beyond recognition,
he's the perfect candidate for an experimental

00:04:55.919 --> 00:04:58.370
program that turns him into a cybernetic super-cop.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:03.100
Naturally, there's more going on with the
RoboCop program than meets the eye, and soon

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.860
enough, Murphy sets out to bring down the
big bads, dead or alive.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.979
Admittedly, Kinnaman often has even less emotion
than Peter Weller, and the PG-13 rating means

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:17.159
that the original’s over-the-top violence
has been toned down considerably in the name

00:05:17.159 --> 00:05:18.590
of making a buck.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:24.229
Still, the 2014 film is surprisingly thoughtful,
focusing focusing on the widespread use—and

00:05:24.229 --> 00:05:29.659
danger—of drone warfare and the increasing
militarization of urban police forces.

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:33.620
The satirical news sequences from the original
have been swapped out with Fox News-style

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.590
segments featuring an angry Samuel L. Jackson,
and there’s an interesting update to Alex

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.110
Murphy’s character arc.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:46.840
In the 1987 film, Murphy starts off as a cold-blooded
cyborg who slowly regains his humanity.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.970
But Padilha puts a nice twist on the story,
with an emotional Murphy becoming more and

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.599
more machine as the movie goes on, losing
his ability to connect with others.

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:01.360
The film also grapples with heady concepts
like free will, and while it lags in a few

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.979
places, it features an amazing in-the-dark
shoot-out and a truly horrific sequence where

00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:09.789
Alex Murphy sees what's left of his body after
a near-fatal explosion.

00:06:09.789 --> 00:06:12.420
It’s never going to replace the original,
but…

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.940
“I’d buy that for a dollar!”

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.120
The Last Boy Scout

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.099
Tony Scott’s The Last Boy Scout had an infamously
troubled production, thanks in part to the

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:28.729
enormous number of rewrites that screenwriter
Shane Black was forced to make.

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:33.159
As a result, we're left with a movie that's
messy, disjointed, and nowhere near as tight

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:36.419
as Black’s other work, like Lethal Weapon
or The Nice Guys.

00:06:36.419 --> 00:06:41.300
That said, while it might be a little jumbled,
The Last Boy Scout is a fascinating slice

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:47.219
of action cinema, a movie full of insane little
moments involving car bombs, helicopter blades,

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:49.120
and foul-mouthed cat puppets.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.669
It starts off with one of the most compelling
openings you'll ever see in an action movie,

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:57.669
with a drug-addled football player pulling
a pistol in the middle of a game.

00:06:57.669 --> 00:07:02.360
From there, we're introduced to our heroes:
Cynical private investigator Joe Hallenbeck,

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:07.931
played by Bruce Willis, and has-been quarterback
Jimmy Dix, played by Damon Wayans, investigate

00:07:07.931 --> 00:07:12.029
the brutal murder of Dix's girlfriend, a dancer
played by Halle Berry.

00:07:12.029 --> 00:07:17.699
As the duo dig deeper into the case, they
uncover a plot involving gambling and the

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:22.189
NFL, but what's really important here is that
Shane Black's dialogue is still clever as

00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:28.160
ever and the dynamic between Willis and Wayans
is crackling with frenemy energy.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.860
“Leather pants?”

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.620
“Yeah.”

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:37.460
It has more than a bit of a problem when it
comes to its female characters, but on the

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:43.199
positive side, Taylor Negron shows up as one
of the very best Shane Black villains, and

00:07:43.199 --> 00:07:48.539
we're treated to a scene in which Bruce Willis
murks a dude with the palm of his hand.

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:49.930
Anaconda

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:56.229
With a 38 percent approval rating on Rotten
Tomatoes, 1997's Anaconda is generally considered

00:07:56.229 --> 00:07:57.440
pretty awful.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:00.150
Critic Mick LaSalle slammed the film by saying,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.529
"Anaconda is about a snake that eats everybody.

00:08:03.529 --> 00:08:05.139
That about says it all."

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:10.620
Time Out London’s review added that the
snake doesn’t just eat people, it screams.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.779
Steve Newton of The Georgia Straight also
piled on, writing,

00:08:13.779 --> 00:08:19.340
"Just to show, early on, how much danger these
folks are in, we get to see the film's titular

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.789
star squeeze a black panther so tight that
one of its eyeballs pop out."

00:08:23.789 --> 00:08:26.210
We’re not really seeing why this is a problem.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:30.659
That’s exactly the kind of stuff that makes
Anaconda so amazing.

00:08:30.659 --> 00:08:34.120
If you go into the movie expecting something
classy, you might walk away disappointed,

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:38.870
but if you're expecting the best B-movie ever,
then you'll have the time of your life.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:42.971
It’s the kind of movie where a guy falling
from a waterfall gets snatched out of the

00:08:42.971 --> 00:08:47.870
air by a snake hanging from a tree branch,
and where you can watch a guy get swallowed

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.820
alive from inside the anaconda itself.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.790
In addition to the killer creatures, there's
Jon Voight as Paul Sarone, a madman who chews

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:57.900
up all the scenery in sight.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.270
Every one of his lines is an over-the-top
gem, and he strangles somebody with his own

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:06.740
legs before dousing Jennifer Lopez with a
bucket of monkey blood.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.470
What's not to love?

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.810
Snow White and the Huntsman

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:15.420
2012's Snow White and the Huntsman has been
accused of being “putrid and seemingly endless,”

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.730
“strange and soulless,” and “underwhelming,”
with a “woeful” script... but it's worth

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:23.120
watching for the visuals alone.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.690
Directed by Rupert Sanders, this gritty fantasy
has images on par with something you might

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.540
see in a movie directed by Guillermo del Toro.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.131
There's the battle where knights fight an
army of soldiers made of glass, or the scene

00:09:34.131 --> 00:09:38.940
when Queen Ravenna bathes herself in a milky
brew, right after munching on a bird's heart.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:44.360
A white stag bursts into a thousand butterflies,
and after turning into a flock of ravens,

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.760
Ravenna crawls from a pit of black ooze, surrounded
by the fluttering of dying birds.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.070
With sequences like these, Snow White and
the Huntsman is equal parts dreamland and

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:58.960
nightmare fuel, and it doesn't hurt that Charlize
Theron throws herself into playing the world's

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:03.450
most evil witch, cranking up the volume and
turning on the terror every time she walks

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:04.500
on-screen.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:09.230
While Theron is wonderfully nuts as Queen
Ravenna, Kristen Stewart, the most underrated

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:13.390
actress in Hollywood, anchors the film with
a much more realistic and empathetic performance

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:14.690
as Snow White.

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:19.220
Even Chris Hemsworth steps up his acting game,
and while it would've been preferable if they'd

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:24.050
used actual little people, the dwarfs are
played by some of the very best British character

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:29.230
actors, like Bob Hoskins, Nick Frost, and
Ian McShane, and they lighten things up every

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.330
time they appear.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:35.380
Sure, it doesn't compare to the Walt Disney
classic, but then it's not really trying to.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:39.620
This is a Snow White movie that's more about
the dark side of fairy tales—the blood,

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.600
the mud, the horror, and the wonder.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.330
Clash of the Titans

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:48.590
With reviews like New York Magazine’s, which
said “It isn’t a train wreck—a train

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:54.310
wreck would be memorable,” 2010's Clash
of the Titans racked up a miserable 28% rating

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:58.520
on Rotten Tomatoes, and went down as a mistake
of mythological proportions.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:02.660
Even the star, Sam Worthington said the movie
"let down some people."

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:06.420
But honestly, if the critics didn’t like
it, that might’ve been because they lost

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:07.730
their sense of fun.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.490
Clash of the Titans is a good, old-fashioned
B-movie, a throwback to a time when Hollywood

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:17.230
made adventure flicks that could charm you
with crazy creatures, over-the-top set pieces,

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:18.990
and a whole lot of imagination.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.870
The story itself is pretty simple.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:26.610
Mankind is getting sick of the gods bossing
them around, so they decide it's time to rebel.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:31.240
Naturally, the gods don't approve, so they
threaten to unleash the monstrous Kraken if

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.880
the human king doesn't sacrifice his daughter.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:38.870
Desperate, the king turns to a demigod named
Perseus, asking him to find a way to kill

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:39.870
the beast.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:45.700
Along the way, Perseus encounters a murderous
Medusa, some oversized scorpions, and a trio

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:46.810
of witches.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:52.540
He zips in between towers on a Pegasus, teams
up with a group of desert-dwelling jinn, and

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:58.300
is forced to deal with all-powerful gods like
Liam Neeson's Zeus and Hades, play by Ralph

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:01.260
Fiennes, who turn the Shakespeare up to 11.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.640
Mix that in with a bunch of monsters and magic,
and you can see how Clash of the Titans is

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:09.930
the exactly kind of movie that might inspire
the next George Lucas to make the next Star

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:10.440
Wars.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:11.440
Thanks for watching!

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.220
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.170
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

